## 🗄️ Database Design

### Conventions
- UUID primary keys; `timestamptz` for instants, `date` for calendar days
- `numeric(5,1)` for leave days (supports half days)
- `CHECK` constraints for statuses (easier to migrate than Postgres `ENUM`)
- Every tenant table has `company_id NOT NULL` and `UNIQUE (company_id, id)` so children can use **composite foreign keys**

### Entity relationships

```
companies 1───* users 1───1 employees *───1 departments
    │                           │  └── manager_id → employees (self-reference)
    │                           ├──* leave_requests *───1 leave_types *───1 companies
    │                           ├──* leave_balances *───1 leave_types
    │                           └──* attendance_records
    ├──* sessions (via users)
    ├──* notifications (per user)
    ├──* audit_logs
    ├──* outbox_events
    └──* monthly_leave_stats

processed_events   (consumer bookkeeping)
```

### Tables

| Table | Purpose | Key columns & constraints |
|---|---|---|
| **companies** | The tenant | `name`, `slug` (unique), `timezone`, `work_start_time`, `late_grace_minutes`, `status` |
| **users** | Login identity (auth data only) | `company_id`, `email` (unique), `password_hash`, `role` CHECK in (admin, hr, manager, employee), `is_active`, `password_changed_at` |
| **sessions** | One row per refresh token | `user_id`, `token_hash` (unique, SHA-256), `family_id`, `expires_at`, `revoked_at`, `replaced_by_id`, `user_agent`, `ip_address` |
| **departments** | Company departments | `UNIQUE (company_id, name)` |
| **employees** | HR record | `user_id` (unique), `employee_code` UNIQUE per company, `department_id`, `manager_id` (self), `job_title`, `date_joined`, `status`; composite FKs `(company_id, department_id)`, `(company_id, manager_id)`; `CHECK (manager_id <> id)` |
| **leave_types** | Company leave policy | `code` (ANNUAL/SICK/UNPAID), `is_paid`, `requires_balance`, `default_days_per_year`, `max_carry_forward_days`; `UNIQUE (company_id, code)` |
| **leave_balances** | Per employee / type / year | `allocated_days`, `carried_forward_days`, `used_days`, `pending_days`; `UNIQUE (employee_id, leave_type_id, year)`; `CHECK (used_days + pending_days <= allocated_days + carried_forward_days)` |
| **leave_requests** | Requests + full history | `start_date`, `end_date`, `days_requested`, `status` (pending/approved/rejected/cancelled), `decided_by`, `decided_at`, `decision_note`; **exclusion constraint** against overlaps |
| **attendance_records** | One row per employee per day | `work_date` (company-local), `check_in_at`, `check_out_at`, `worked_minutes`, `is_late`, `late_minutes`; `UNIQUE (employee_id, work_date)`; `CHECK (check_out_at > check_in_at)` |
| **notifications** | In-app inbox | `user_id`, `type`, `title`, `body`, `entity_type`, `entity_id`, `read_at`, `source_event_id` (unique → no duplicates) |
| **audit_logs** | Append-only audit trail | `actor_user_id`, `action`, `entity_type`, `entity_id`, `old_values` jsonb, `new_values` jsonb, `ip_address`, `request_id`; app DB role has no UPDATE/DELETE |
| **outbox_events** | Events awaiting publish | `topic`, `message_key`, `event_type`, `payload` jsonb, `published_at`, `attempts`, `last_error` |
| **processed_events** | Idempotent consumers | PK `(consumer_group, event_id)` |
| **monthly_leave_stats** | Reporting aggregates | PK `(company_id, year, month, leave_type_id)`; `approved_requests`, `approved_days` |

### The two most important constraints

**1. No overlapping leave — enforced by the database**
```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS btree_gist;

ALTER TABLE leave_requests ADD CONSTRAINT no_overlapping_leave
  EXCLUDE USING gist (
    employee_id WITH =,
    daterange(start_date, end_date, '[]') WITH &&
  ) WHERE (status IN ('pending', 'approved'));
```
An application-level "check then insert" has a race: two simultaneous requests both pass the check. The constraint makes the second insert fail atomically.

**2. Balance can never go negative**
```sql
CHECK (used_days + pending_days <= allocated_days + carried_forward_days)
```

### Key indexes

| Index | Serves |
|---|---|
| `employees (company_id, status, last_name, id)` | Employee list with filter + keyset pagination |
| `employees (company_id, manager_id)` | "My team" / approval routing |
| `leave_requests (company_id, status, created_at)` | Pending approvals dashboard |
| `leave_requests (company_id, employee_id, start_date DESC)` | Leave history |
| `attendance_records (company_id, work_date)` | Daily attendance report |
| `notifications (user_id, created_at DESC) WHERE read_at IS NULL` | Unread badge & inbox |
| `audit_logs (company_id, created_at DESC)` | Audit log screen |
| `outbox_events (created_at) WHERE published_at IS NULL` | Relay polling |

### Concurrency-safe leave approval

```
BEGIN;
  SET LOCAL app.company_id = $companyId;
  SELECT * FROM leave_requests WHERE id = $1 FOR UPDATE;       -- lock request
     → status <> 'pending' ? ROLLBACK + 409 Conflict
  SELECT * FROM leave_balances WHERE … FOR UPDATE;             -- lock balance
  UPDATE leave_balances SET pending_days = pending_days - d,
                            used_days    = used_days + d;
  UPDATE leave_requests SET status = 'approved', decided_by = …, decided_at = now();
  INSERT INTO audit_logs (…);
  INSERT INTO outbox_events (… 'leave.approved' …);
COMMIT;
```
Two managers clicking Approve simultaneously: the second blocks on the row lock, then sees `approved` and receives **409**. Locks are always taken in the same order (request → balance) to avoid deadlocks.

---

## 🔐 Multi-Tenancy & Tenant Isolation

**Model:** shared database, shared schema, `company_id` on every tenant table.

| Approach | Isolation | Operational cost | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Database per tenant | Strongest | N databases, N migrations | Overkill |
| Schema per tenant | Strong | N migrations, pooling pain | Overkill |
| **Shared schema + `company_id`** | Enforced in 3 layers below | One schema | ✅ Chosen |

### Three layers of defence

1. **Application layer**
   - `companyId` comes **only** from the verified access token (`req.auth.companyId`) — never from the URL, query or body.
   - Every repository function requires `companyId` and includes it in every `WHERE`.
   - Cross-tenant lookups return **404**, not 403 (don't reveal existence).

2. **Schema layer — composite foreign keys**
   - `FOREIGN KEY (company_id, employee_id) REFERENCES employees (company_id, id)`
   - The database rejects a leave request, attendance row or manager link that points to another company's employee — even if application code is buggy.

3. **Database layer — Row-Level Security**
   ```sql
   ALTER TABLE employees ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
   ALTER TABLE employees FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
   CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON employees
     USING (company_id = current_setting('app.company_id')::uuid);
   ```
   - `withTransaction()` runs `SET LOCAL app.company_id = …` at the start of every transaction.
   - `SET LOCAL` (not `SET`) is essential with connection pooling — otherwise the tenant leaks to the next request on that connection.
   - `FORCE` is required because table owners bypass RLS by default.
   - Migrations run as a separate owner role; the app connects as a non-owner role.

Proven by `tests/integration/tenant-isolation.test.js`: company B's token must never read, update or reference company A's data via any endpoint.

---

## 📨 Events & Kafka

### Why the transactional outbox

Publishing to Kafka after `COMMIT` can lose events if the process crashes between the two steps (the **dual-write problem**). Instead, the event row is inserted into `outbox_events` **in the same transaction** as the business change, and a relay publishes it:

```
API transaction:  UPDATE leave_requests … ; INSERT INTO outbox_events … ; COMMIT
Relay (loop):     SELECT … WHERE published_at IS NULL ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 100 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
                  → producer.send() → UPDATE outbox_events SET published_at = now()
```
If the relay crashes after sending but before marking → the event is sent again → **at-least-once** → consumers must be idempotent.

### Topics

| Topic | Partitions | Message key | Events | Consumer groups |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `leave-events` | 3 | `leave_request_id` | `leave.requested`, `leave.approved`, `leave.rejected`, `leave.cancelled` | `notifications`, `reporting` |
| `employee-events` | 3 | `employee_id` | `employee.created` | `notifications` |
| `attendance-events` | 3 | `employee_id` | `attendance.recorded` | `reporting` |
| `leave-events.dlt`, `employee-events.dlt`, `attendance-events.dlt` | 1 | same | messages that failed after max retries | manual / replay script |

- **Ordering:** guaranteed only within a partition → keying by `leave_request_id` keeps `requested` before `approved` for the same request.
- **Consumer groups:** `notifications` and `reporting` each receive every `leave-events` message independently; within a group, partitions are split across worker instances.

### Event envelope

```json
{
  "eventId": "uuid (= outbox_events.id)",
  "eventType": "leave.approved",
  "companyId": "uuid",
  "occurredAt": "2026-11-13T10:00:00Z",
  "version": 1,
  "payload": { "leaveRequestId": "…", "employeeId": "…", "approvedBy": "…", "days": 3 }
}
```

### Consumer processing rules

1. Begin DB transaction
2. `INSERT INTO processed_events (consumer_group, event_id)` — duplicate key ⇒ already handled ⇒ skip
3. Do the work (insert notification / upsert stats)
4. Commit DB transaction
5. Commit Kafka offset (**after** the DB commit — never before)
6. On error: retry with exponential backoff (3 attempts) → then publish to `<topic>.dlt` and commit offset so the partition isn't blocked

Emails are sent **after** the DB commit; a crash between "commit" and "send" is handled by a `email_sent_at` flag on the notification and a retry sweep (documented trade-off: email is at-least-once).

---

## ⚡ Redis Usage

| Use | Key pattern | TTL | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Company settings cache | `company:{id}:settings` | 10 min | Invalidated on update |
| Leave types cache | `company:{id}:leave-types` | 10 min | Invalidated on policy change |
| Dashboard counts | `company:{id}:dashboard` | 60 s | Short TTL; slight staleness acceptable |
| Login rate limit | `rl:login:{ip}` / `rl:login:{email}` | 15 min | 5 attempts |
| Password reset rate limit | `rl:reset:{email}` | 1 h | 3 requests |
| Password reset token | `reset:{sha256(token)}` → userId | 15 min | `GETDEL` = single use |
| Scheduled-job lock | `lock:job:yearly-allocation` | job duration | **Only** distributed lock in the system |

**Cache strategy:** cache-aside — read from Redis, on miss read Postgres and populate; on write, update Postgres **then** delete the key. Redis is never the source of truth; if Redis is down, reads fall back to Postgres and rate limiting fails **closed** for login (deny) and **open** elsewhere (documented trade-off).

**Why no Redis lock for leave approval:** Postgres row locks (`FOR UPDATE`) are transactional, released automatically on commit/rollback, and live next to the data. A Redis lock would add a second system that can disagree with the database.

---

## 🛠️ Build Guide — Step 1 to Step 20

> One module at a time. Each step ends with a ✅ **definition of done**. Don't start the next step until the current one passes its tests. Commit after every step.

### Phase 0 — Foundations

**Step 1 · Prerequisites**
Node.js 22+, Docker Desktop, Git, a Postgres client (psql / TablePlus / DBeaver), an AWS account (needed from Step 19).
✅ `node -v`, `docker compose version` work.

**Step 2 · Repository & tooling**
Create `server/` and `client/`, ESLint + Prettier, `.gitignore`, `.env.example`, Jest config.
✅ `npm run lint` and `npm test` (empty) succeed.

**Step 3 · Local infrastructure (Docker Compose)**
`infra/docker-compose.yml` with Postgres 16, Redis 7, Kafka (KRaft, single broker), Kafka UI, Mailpit. Script to create topics + DLTs.
✅ All containers healthy; topics visible in Kafka UI (http://localhost:8085).

**Step 4 · Server skeleton**
`config/env.js` (Zod-validated env, fail fast), `logger.js`, `app.js` / `server.js` split, `requestId`, `errorHandler`, `AppError` classes, `validate` middleware, `/health/live` + `/health/ready` (checks Postgres & Redis), graceful shutdown (stop accepting → finish in-flight → close pool/Redis/Kafka).
✅ `/health/ready` returns 200; unknown route returns a consistent JSON 404; `Ctrl+C` shuts down cleanly.

**Step 5 · Database setup & migrations**
`db/pool.js`, `withTransaction()` helper, `node-pg-migrate`. Create **all core tables, constraints and indexes** from the design above. Seed script with two demo companies.
✅ `npm run migrate:up` and `migrate:down` both work; constraints verified manually in psql (try inserting an overlapping leave → rejected).

### Phase 1 — Identity & tenancy

**Step 6 · Auth module — registration & login**
Company registration (creates company + admin user + employee + default leave types in **one transaction**), login, bcrypt, access token (15 min), refresh token (7 days, httpOnly cookie, hashed in `sessions`).
✅ Register → login → `GET /auth/me` works; wrong password returns generic error.

**Step 7 · Auth module — refresh rotation, logout, sessions, rate limiting**
Rotation with **reuse detection** (revoke whole `family_id`), logout, list & revoke sessions, Redis login rate limiting.
✅ Reusing an old refresh token revokes the family; 6th failed login → 429.

**Step 8 · Auth module — password reset & RBAC**
Forgot/reset password (Redis token, `GETDEL`, revoke all sessions after reset, email via Mailpit), `authorize(...roles)` middleware.
✅ Reset link works once only; employee hitting an admin route → 403.

**Step 9 · Tenant isolation hardening (RLS)**
Separate migration role vs app role, RLS policies + `FORCE`, `SET LOCAL app.company_id` in `withTransaction`. Write `tenant-isolation.test.js`.
✅ Isolation tests pass; deliberately removing a `WHERE company_id` in a repository still returns no foreign rows.

### Phase 2 — Core HR

**Step 10 · Company & department modules**
Company settings (timezone, work start, grace), department CRUD, Redis cache-aside for settings.
✅ Settings cached and invalidated on update (verify with `redis-cli MONITOR`).

**Step 11 · Employee module**
Create (creates user + employee in one transaction, emits `employee.created` via outbox), update, deactivate (also revokes sessions), assign department/manager/role (audit-logged), profile, search + filters + **keyset pagination**.
✅ Role change appears in audit log; pagination stable while data changes; manager-cycle prevented.

**Step 12 · Audit module**
`writeAudit()` used inside existing transactions; read API with filters for admin/HR.
✅ Every create/update/role change/admin action produces exactly one audit row with before/after.

### Phase 3 — Leave & attendance

**Step 13 · Leave module — policies & balances**
Leave types per company, yearly allocation (scheduled job with Redis lock), carry-forward, balance endpoint.
✅ Balances created for all active employees; job running twice concurrently allocates once.

**Step 14 · Leave module — apply, approve, reject, cancel**
Working-day calculation in company timezone, pending-day reservation, overlap constraint → 409, concurrency-safe approval (row locks), cancel pending, history, audit + outbox events.
✅ `leave-concurrency.test.js`: 10 parallel approvals → exactly 1 succeeds; overlapping apply → 409; balance never negative.

**Step 15 · Attendance module**
Check-in/out (company timezone), worked minutes, late detection, daily & monthly reports, `attendance.recorded` event.
✅ Double check-in → 409; late flag correct around timezone/DST boundaries (unit tests).

### Phase 4 — Async processing

**Step 16 · Outbox relay & Kafka consumers**
Relay worker (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`), consumer factory with retries + DLT, `processed_events` idempotency, notification worker (in-app + email), reporting worker (`monthly_leave_stats`).
✅ Kill a worker mid-processing → restart → no duplicate notifications; a poison message lands in the DLT; two notification workers split partitions.

**Step 17 · Notifications & reports APIs**
Inbox (unread count, mark read), dashboard endpoint (cached 60 s), monthly leave & attendance summaries.
✅ Approving leave produces an in-app notification + Mailpit email within seconds.

### Phase 5 — Frontend

**Step 18 · React SaaS dashboard**
App shell (sidebar, topbar, notification bell), Axios refresh interceptor (single in-flight refresh), protected & role-gated routes, reusable UI kit, pages: login/register/reset, dashboard, employees, departments, my leave, apply leave, approvals, attendance, reports, audit log, profile/sessions/settings. Loading, empty and error states everywhere.
✅ Full user journey for each role works end-to-end in the browser.

### Phase 6 — Ship it

**Step 19 · Dockerise & deploy to AWS**
Multi-stage Dockerfiles, `docker-compose.prod.yml`, RDS PostgreSQL (private subnet), EC2 running Nginx + API + workers + Redis + Kafka containers, HTTPS (Let's Encrypt), secrets in SSM Parameter Store / `.env` on host (not in Git).
✅ App reachable over HTTPS; `/health/ready` green; data persists across redeploys.

**Step 20 · CI/CD, documentation, polish**
GitHub Actions (lint → test with service containers → build images → deploy via SSH), architecture diagram, ER diagram, API docs, ADRs, screenshots, demo video.
✅ Push to `main` deploys automatically; README complete.

